    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/bayinnaung"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bayinnaung.jpg" alt="Bayinnaung" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/bayinnaung">Bayinnaung</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bayinnaung.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Phyo WP, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Restored Toungoo Kingdom, also called the Nyaungyan dynasty after its founder, rose from the wreckage of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-toungoo-empire">First Toungoo Empire</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayinnaung">Bayinnaung</a> (r. 1550–1581) had built the largest empire in the history of mainland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>, but under his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanda-bayin">Nanda Bayin</a> it disintegrated: Siam broke free, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-burma">Lower Burma</a> was depopulated by war and famine, and in 1599 the capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bago">Pegu</a> was sacked by the forces of Toungoo and Arakan. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-burma">Upper Burma</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nyaungyan-min">Nyaungyan Min</a>, a son of Bayinnaung, had meanwhile gathered power at Ava, and from the late 1590s he rebuilt royal authority over the dry zone and the nearby Shan states. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anaukpetlun">Anaukpetlun</a> (r. 1605–1628) completed the restoration. He took Prome in 1608 and Toungoo in 1610, and in 1613 captured Syriam, the port held by the Portuguese adventurer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/filipe-de-brito-e-nicote">Filipe de Brito e Nicote</a>, who was executed. Anaukpetlun then attacked the Siamese positions on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanintharyi-region">Tenasserim</a> coast without lasting success, and in 1614–1615 retook Chiang Mai, restoring Lan Na to Burmese overlordship. He ruled from Pegu, symbolically reclaiming the seat of Bayinnaung. The new state was smaller than its predecessor, having abandoned its claims to Siam and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a>, but it reunited the Irrawaddy basin and its Shan and Lan Na dependencies under a single dynasty.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/kaunghmudaw-pagoda"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kaunghmudaw-pagoda.jpg" alt="Kaunghmudaw Pagoda" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/kaunghmudaw-pagoda">Kaunghmudaw Pagoda</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kaunghmudaw_Pagoda.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Daniel Julie, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The kingdom’s economic heart was the irrigated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> land of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-burma">Upper Burma</a>, above all the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyaukse">Kyaukse</a> district and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minbu">Minbu</a> and Mu valley areas, which fed the capital and the royal service population. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-burma">Lower Burma</a>, depopulated by the wars of the late sixteenth century, slowly recovered as a rice-growing and trading region, with Syriam as the main port. Through it passed exports of lac, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>, precious stones, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> and rice, and imports of Indian cloth, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> and firearms. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a> kept a trading post in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> from 1635 to 1679 and English traders were active intermittently, while overland caravans carried Burmese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunnan">Yunnan</a> in exchange for Chinese goods. Society was divided between crown servicemen (<em>ahmudan</em>), who held land in return for military or labour service, and commoners (<em>athi</em>), who paid taxes, with debt-bondsmen and religious servants attached to monasteries and pagodas. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theravada-buddhism">Theravada Buddhism</a> framed royal legitimacy and village life: kings sponsored monasteries, the purification of the monkhood and great pagodas such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thalun">Thalun</a>’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaunghmudaw-pagoda">Kaunghmudaw</a> at Sagaing, completed in 1648. Monastic schools gave Burma unusually high rates of male literacy for the period, and court literature, legal compilations and chronicles flourished, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/u-kala">U Kala</a>’s great chronicle, the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maha-yazawin">Maha Yazawin</a></em>, completed in the early eighteenth century.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/wu-sangui"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wu-sangui.jpg" alt="Wu Sangui" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/wu-sangui">Wu Sangui</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wu_Sangui.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Author in Qing Dynasty, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The army rested on the <em>ahmudan</em> system, which bound regiments of hereditary servicemen, settled on crown land near the capital, to provide soldiers, boatmen, cavalry and elephant corps when called. These were supplemented by levies from the provinces and the Shan states. Foreign gunners formed a valued specialist corps: after the fall of Syriam in 1613, Portuguese and Eurasian prisoners were resettled in villages of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-burma">Upper Burma</a> as hereditary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musket">musketeers</a> and artillerymen, the Catholic <em>bayingyi</em> communities whose descendants survive today. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anaukpetlun">Anaukpetlun</a>’s campaigns of 1606–1615 against Prome, Toungoo, Syriam and Lan Na were the dynasty’s main wars of conquest. Thereafter the kingdom fought mainly defensive wars. Between 1658 and 1662 Ming loyalist forces, following the fugitive <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yongli-emperor">Yongli Emperor</a> into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a>, raided the country, and in 1662 a Qing army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wu-sangui">Wu Sangui</a> reached Ava, withdrawing only after the Burmese court handed over the emperor. From the 1720s cavalry raids from Manipur under Raja <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamheiba">Gharib Nawaz</a> ravaged the dry zone, reaching Sagaing, across the river from Ava, in the late 1730s, and the court proved unable to stop them. In 1740 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mon-people">Mon</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-burma">Lower Burma</a> rebelled, and the forces of the Restored Hanthawaddy Kingdom, after a decade of fighting, advanced up the Irrawaddy and took Ava in 1752.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/thalun"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thalun.jpg" alt="Thalun" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/thalun">Thalun</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Statute_of_King_Thalun.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Kantabon, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victor-lieberman">Victor Lieberman</a> has described the Restored Toungoo state as considerably more centralised than the loose empire of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayinnaung">Bayinnaung</a>. Rather than assigning great provinces to royal brothers and sons who could rebel, as earlier kings had done, the Nyaungyan rulers increasingly kept princes at the capital and governed the core through appointed officials and hereditary local headmen (<em>myothugyi</em>). <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thalun">Thalun</a> (r. 1629–1648) consolidated this system. In 1635 he moved the capital from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bago">Pegu</a> back to Ava, closer to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> lands and manpower of the dry zone, and in 1638 he ordered a revenue inquest (<em>sittan</em>) recording the population, service obligations and dues of each locality, which became a model for later surveys. Royal service groups were reorganised and expanded, and limits were placed on the drift of crown servicemen into private debt bondage or the service of monasteries. Central government was conducted through the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hluttaw">Hluttaw</a></em>, the council of chief ministers, and a network of secretaries and treasury officials, while Buddhist law books (<em>dhammathat</em>) and royal edicts governed justice. The Shan states and Lan Na were ruled indirectly through their own princes, or <em>saopha</em>, and governors, who sent tribute and hostages. After Thalun the system gradually weakened as monastic landholdings grew and court factions undermined weaker kings.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/hmannan-yazawin"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hmannan-yazawin.jpg" alt="Hmannan Yazawin" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/hmannan-yazawin">Hmannan Yazawin</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Glass_Palace_Chronicle.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Hybernator, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Restored Toungoo Kingdom fixed the shape of the Burmese state for the next two centuries. By concentrating power in the Irrawaddy valley and governing through service groups, revenue inquests and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hluttaw">Hluttaw</a>, it created the administrative template inherited and expanded by the Konbaung dynasty. Its shift of the capital from coastal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bago">Pegu</a> to inland Ava confirmed the dominance of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-burma">Upper Burma</a> over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mon-people">Mon</a> south and reinforced a gradual spread of Burman language and identity in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-burma">Lower Burma</a>, though Mon resentment of northern rule fuelled the rebellion of 1740. The kingdom’s relative peace in the seventeenth century allowed a flowering of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theravada-buddhism">Theravada</a> scholarship, monastic reform debates and Burmese-language literature, including legal texts and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/u-kala">U Kala</a>’s chronicle, on which later works such as the Konbaung <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hmannan-yazawin">Hmannan Yazawin</a></em> drew. Its dealings with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> and English companies were among the first sustained contacts between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> and European trading firms, and the Catholic gunner villages of the Mu valley remain a distinctive legacy. The kingdom’s handling of the Ming refugee court and the Qing army also placed Burma within the wider politics of the Ming–Qing transition. Historians now tend to see the period not as a mere decline after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayinnaung">Bayinnaung</a> but as a phase of consolidation that made the later Konbaung expansion possible.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/alaungpaya"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alaungpaya.jpg" alt="Alaungpaya" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/alaungpaya">Alaungpaya</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alaungpaya.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Phyo WP, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The kingdom’s difficulties in the eighteenth century combined administrative erosion with external attack. Crown servicemen increasingly escaped their duties by becoming private clients or monastery dependants, reducing the manpower and revenue at the king’s disposal, while court factions dominated weak rulers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahadhammayaza-dipati">Mahadhammayaza Dipati</a> (r. 1733–1752). In 1727 Chiang Mai rebelled and Lan Na slipped from Burmese control. From the 1720s Manipuri horsemen raided deep into the dry zone, burning towns and monasteries, and the court’s failure to stop them badly damaged its prestige. In 1740 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mon-people">Mon</a> and other groups in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-burma">Lower Burma</a> rose at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bago">Pegu</a> and proclaimed their own king, founding the Restored Hanthawaddy Kingdom. After years of inconclusive war, the southern army of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/binnya-dala">Binnya Dala</a> advanced north and captured Ava in 1752. The king and his family were carried off to Pegu, where he was later killed, and the Nyaungyan dynasty came to an end. Northern resistance did not collapse, however. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alaungpaya">Alaungpaya</a>, the headman of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shwebo">Moksobo</a>, refused to submit, rallied the villages of the dry zone and within a few years drove out the southern forces, founding the Konbaung dynasty that reunited the country on the foundations the Restored Toungoo kings had laid.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Restored Toungoo Kingdom rebuilt the Burmese state after the collapse of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayinnaung">Bayinnaung</a>’s empire, creating a more centralised order based at Ava, whose royal service groups, revenue inquests and ministerial council outlived its fall in 1752 and became the foundation of the Konbaung kingdom.</em></p>

